   290	
   291	## D3 — Audit re-base
   292	
   293	### Per-check disposition
   294	
   295	| legacy check | disposition | arrival replacement |
   296	|---|---|---|
   297	| `index` (`:340-353`) | TRANSLATE unchanged | index exists and is readable |
   298	| `_check_offset` (`:430-461`) | **DISSOLVE → re-found** | `_check_consumed`: the index's stamped `ResumeMark` (`arrival_lineage`, `arrival_ordinal`) vs the log's head ordinal. Claim: "index is behind arrival by N record(s), consumed through ordinal X." |
   299	| `_suffix_unindexed` (`:383-427`) | **DISSOLVE, no replacement** | Its job was corroborating an unverifiable byte marker (finding 4). The mark's ordinal is structurally verified by `walk_from`'s anchor check. `beyond_offset` on `Check` dies with it. |
   300	| `_check_counts` (`:464-492`) | TRANSLATE + strengthen | Stamped counters vs `COUNT(*)` as today, plus: no row may carry `arrival_ordinal >` the consumed ordinal, and (arrival-canonical only) no row may carry a NULL `arrival_ordinal`. Both are out-of-band-insert location claims. |
   301	| `_check_last_line` (`:495-542`) | TRANSLATE | `_check_consumed_edge`: read the record at the consumed ordinal via the log, expand it with `rows_of_record`, and `row_matches` every row against the index. Same detection (an out-of-band edit to the last consumed row), no byte seek. `_last_line`'s backward-scan helper (`:290-310`) dissolves with it. |
   302	| jsonl comparison (`--deep`, `_deep_checks:605-704`) | **wire, do not write** | `arrival_projection.audit_derived_log` — set membership over digests, already built. |
   303	
   304	**Byte offsets do not survive as custody claims anywhere.** `arrival_offset`
   305	remains inside the `ResumeMark` because arrival's own verified walk uses it
   306	as a seek hint, checked against the anchor record before it is trusted. That
   307	is a substrate internal, not an audit claim, and no `Check` may cite it.
   308	
   309	### Required mechanism — how L1 stays cheap (fence for the implementer)
   310	
   311	`ArrivalLog.read(ordinal)` is a **full verified walk from zero** by design
   312	(`arrival.py:1040-1053`: "the walk is the only way in on purpose"). An
   313	implementer who reaches for `read(consumed_ordinal)` turns the default gate
   314	into an O(n) per-record-hash verification of the whole log and silently
   315	destroys the cheapness this section claims. The cheap path is
   316	`walk_marked(stamped_mark)`: the mark's byte offset seeks directly to its
   317	anchor record, whose ordinal and chain linkage are verified there
   318	(`arrival.py:1165-1174`), and the iterator then yields only the suffix.
   319	`_check_consumed_edge` reads the consumed record through that anchor;
   320	`_check_consumed` counts the suffix, so it costs O(records behind), which is
   321	zero on a healthy store. **L1 must not call `ArrivalLog.read` or
   322	`ArrivalLog.walk`.** G-D3-2 asserts this.
   323	
   324	### The L1 / `--deep` split under the new model
   325	
   326	- **L1 (`audit_agreement`)** — O(1) plus the size of one record:
   327	  `index` · `consumed` · `counts` · `consumed-edge`. It answers from the
   328	  arrival log's head and the index's stamped mark. It never reads a byte
   329	  offset and never streams the log.
   330	- **`--deep` (`audit_deep`)** — L1, then a full `ArrivalLog.walk()` (which
   331	  verifies density, record hashes, ordinals and chain linkage as a free
   332	  consequence of walking), row-by-row index comparison over
   333	  `rows_of_record`, `audit_derived_log` for the derived `.jsonl`, and the
   334	  tick chain re-derived from log content as today. `verify_authorship` is
   335	  available at this tier but is a separate verb, not folded in — it makes an
   336	  authorship claim, not an agreement claim.
   337	
   338	### Scope-the-claim discipline
   339	
   340	Every `Check` emits a **location claim**. Concretely: `beyond_offset: bool`
   341	(a flag one step from innocence) is replaced by `behind_by: int` and
   342	`at_ordinal: int` — coordinates, not verdicts. No check may return a message
   343	asserting a store is intact, clean, or safe; the strongest positive form
   344	stays the existing one, "N fact(s), M tick(s) accounted for". The L1 pass
   345	returning all-true means *these four questions found nothing here*, and the
   346	report's own prose must not let a renderer say more.
   347	
   348	### Blast radius — the one call site
   349	
   350	`apps/loops/src/loops/commands/store.py:141-163` gates on
   351	`is_jsonl_canonical(canonical)` and returns `None` for an arrival store, so
   352	today arrival stores are simply unaudited. That gate growing an arrival arm
   353	**is** the "blast radius one call site" the plan names. Note for the fence:
   354	this is `apps/`, and the arc's scope law says apps/ is diff-empty across
   355	every slice. Flagged as **open question D3-Q1** — either D touches this one
   356	dispatch as a ruled exception, or the audit lands library-side and the CLI
   357	arm waits for the tail's CLI rebuild, leaving arrival stores unaudited
   358	through the CLI in the interim. Recommended: **library-side complete, plus
   359	the one dispatch arm as a ruled, receipted exception** — an audit surface
   360	nothing can reach is not a surface.
   361	
