   313	def _derived_digests(canonical: Path) -> set[bytes]:
   314	    """Digest every line the arrival log projects, retaining none of them.
   315	
   316	    Deliberately NOT ``{_digest(line) for line in derived_lines(...)}``:
   317	    :func:`derived_lines` must materialize every line because it SORTS them,
   318	    and an audit that borrowed it inherited a cost it does not need. Here
   319	    each line is produced, hashed, and dropped, so what survives the walk is
   320	    32 bytes per record.
   321	
   322	    The bound, stated exactly rather than loosely: peak is **flat in record
   323	    count** and **linear in the size of the LARGEST single record**. What
   324	    survives the walk is 32 bytes per record; what is in flight is one
   325	    record, which costs a small multiple of its own size because it is
   326	    decoded, re-encoded and hashed. Measured: 5, 10, 20 and 40 records of
   327	    1 MB each all peak at 7.02 MB, while one 4 MB record peaks at 28 MB.
   328	
   329	    So "bounded by record count, never payload size" holds in the sense
   330	    that matters — an audit over a big store does not grow with the store —
   331	    but a single enormous record is still transiently expensive, and saying
   332	    otherwise would be the kind of unbounded promise this module refuses to
   333	    make elsewhere.
   334	    """
